\import{spin-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The real Pin kernel is the scalar pair \citet{Section 1.8, Proposition 1.21, pp. 39--40}{gallier2014clifford}}
\lean/tauceti{TauCeti.CliffordAlgebra.zmodTwoMulEquivKerPinToOrthogonal}

\p{For a real quadratic space of signature #{(p,q)}, the Pin action has the
same two-element kernel:

##{(\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z)_{\mathrm{mult}}
\simeq \ker\bigl(\Pin(p,q)\longrightarrow O(p,q)\bigr).}

Its nontrivial generator is the image in Pin of the scalar Spin element
#{-1}. The identification is obtained by comparing the Pin kernel with the
Spin kernel, not by adding a second pair of central elements. Gallier's real
double-cover proof uses exactly the quotient by #{\{\pm1\}}.}

\p{TauCeti transports the Spin-kernel equivalence to Pin over a field #{K}
when the module is nontrivial and finite-dimensional, #{Q} is nondegenerate,
and #{2} is invertible. These are the formal theorem's exact assumptions; the
cited scholarly claim is the real case.}
